<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis William, Margrave of Baden-Baden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1655">1655</a>–<a href="/years/1707">1707</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Turkish Louis, Türkenlouis, Louis William of Baden-Baden · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis William, Margrave of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> from 1677, was an imperial field marshal known as the Turkish Louis for his victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, above all at Slankamen in 1691. He later commanded imperial armies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. After the French destroyed Baden-Baden in 1689, he moved his residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt-palace">Rastatt Palace</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-william-margrave-of-baden-baden.jpg" alt="Louis William, Margrave of Baden-Baden" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not stated. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_William_(1655-1707).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis William was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 8 April 1655, a son of Hereditary Prince Ferdinand Maximilian of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and his French wife, Louise of Savoy. Louis XIV was his godfather. His parents were estranged, and his father took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where his grandparents raised him. His father died before his grandfather Wilhelm, so that Louis succeeded as reigning margrave and head of the Catholic branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a>. The Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, his mother's brother, was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a>, whose career began in Louis's shadow and who served with him against the French.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Louis William served first under Raimondo Montecuccoli against Turenne, then under the duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and with his uncle Hermann of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> took part in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippsburg">Philippsburg</a> in 1676. At the Turkish siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683 he threw his forces into the city and by a sally joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Jan III Sobieski</a> and the Duke of Lorraine.</p><p>In 1689 he defeated the Turks at Niš and was made chief commander of the Imperial army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, where he won a victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Slankamen in 1691. The Turks called him the red king for his red uniform jacket. He also urged the repair of the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osijek">Osijek</a> and a new fort, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrda">Tvrđa</a>, on Vauban's principles.</p><p>After a fruitless campaign in 1692 he went to the Upper Rhine, and defended it against superior French forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">War of the Grand Alliance</a>. In 1701 he built the Lines of Stollhofen to protect northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> in September 1702 but was beaten by the French under the Duke of Villars at Friedlingen. In 1704 he joined the campaign of Marlborough and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Eugene of Savoy</a>, distinguished himself at the Battle of Schellenberg and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> and Landau, drawing Bavarian troops away from Blenheim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A wound received at Schellenberg in July 1704 never fully healed, and he died of it on 4 January 1707 at the age of 51 in his unfinished Schloss Rastatt. He was buried at the Stiftskirche in Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. His wife, Sibylle of Saxe-Lauenburg, ruled as regent for their son Louis George until October 1727, and built Schloss Favorite as a summer residence in his memory. His daughter Auguste married Louis d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, and their descendant became King Louis Philippe of the French in 1830.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis William, Margrave of Baden-Baden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Louis William of Baden-Baden</b>, the 'Turkish Louis', fought the Ottomans and built Rastatt Palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden">Margraviate of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote>Margrave <b>Louis William</b>, an imperial general known as 'Türkenlouis' for his victories over the Ottomans, saw Baden-Baden burned by the French in 1689 and built a new Baroque residence at Rastatt from 1700.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-baden">Margraviate of Baden-Baden</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1688" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis William, Margrave of Baden-Baden"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1688">What was here in 1688? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
